WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.220
Every screen is read in some order,
whether you planned it or not.

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:07.880
Visual hierarchy is choosing
that order on purpose.

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:12.440
Before you touch a font size, write
down what someone should notice first,

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:17.660
second, and third. By the end of this
lesson you will rank a pricing card,

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:20.860
style that ranking with one
or two levers at a time,

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:22.520
and check it with a blur.

00:00:23.030 --> 00:00:28.360
Take a pricing card. A typical first
draft gives everything equal weight.

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:34.260
Plan name, price, description, feature
list, and button all sit at 16

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:36.160
pixels in the same colour.

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:40.320
The eye has nowhere to land,
so people read top to bottom,

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:41.840
or they give up.

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:45.020
Rank it instead. The price comes first,

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:48.200
because it answers the
question the person came with.

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:52.100
The plan name comes second, so
they know what the price buys.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:54.400
The call to action is third.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:56.300
Everything else is fourth.

00:00:56.740 --> 00:00:58.900
Now you have a brief for the styling.

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:02.100
You only need a few tools
to express a ranking.

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:05.280
Size. Larger reads first.

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:10.340
Keep the jumps clear. Sixteen
pixels next to seventeen is noise.

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:13.700
Sixteen next to thirty-two is a decision.

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:20.820
Weight. A semibold label beside regular text
creates hierarchy without changing size.

00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:25.850
Colour and contrast. Secondary
text in a softer neutral,

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:32.460
for example #737373 on white,
steps back while staying readable.

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:37.860
Position and space. In a left-to-right
language, things at the top left,

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:41.640
and things with more room around
them, get noticed sooner.

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:44.620
Use one or two levers per step down.

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:47.860
Using all four at once usually overshoots.

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:53.220
Here is the before. Four paragraphs
and a button, no classes,

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:55.560
so the browser gives them the same voice.

00:01:55.970 --> 00:01:58.160
The price now carries the card.

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:04.260
Two rem, weight 700. “Per month” drops
back to one rem and the muted colour,

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:06.580
so the number stays the hero.

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:10.580
The plan name is small, but weight
and colour keep it distinct.

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:15.120
The description steps back to the
same neutral, without a size change,

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:17.220
because it is further down the ranking.

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:19.800
One or two levers per step.

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:25.060
A quick test. Blur your eyes, or
apply a heavy blur in devtools,

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:29.960
and look at the screen. The shapes that
still stand out are your real hierarchy.

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:34.620
If they are not the ones on your list,
adjust the levers until they are.

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:37.940
The blur removes the words
and leaves size, weight,

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:39.280
and contrast.

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:42.840
Write the ranking down
before you style anything.

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:46.900
Each step down the ranking should
change one or two properties,

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:51.240
clearly. Test with a blur, not
with your memory of the content.

00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:56.200
Next, those gaps you used to separate the
price from the description get a system of

00:02:56.200 --> 00:03:01.460
their own: an 8 point spacing scale,
and a rule for what belongs together.
